    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (76.11) and **Christian Yelich** (73.09) anchoring the outfield as our premier starts. **Pete Crow-Armstrong** benefits significantly from a favorable matchup against Erick Fedde, where his elite sprint speed and a high likelihood of reaching base translate into critical stolen base upside, further bolstered by an opportunistic SP skill rating. Similarly, **Christian Yelich** leverages a strong platoon advantage against Bailey Ober; despite a wind-in warning at the park, his profile remains elite for today\u2019s requirements. Additionally, **Carson Benge** (67.24) and **Junior Caminero** (61.60) represent high-efficiency plays, with **Carson Benge** specifically primed to exploit the 6.75 xERA of Elmer Rodr\u00edguez, providing the offensive floor needed to sustain our Roto standing.\n\nWe faced a tight squeeze at the MI/SS slots, ultimately favoring **Colt Keith** (55.89) over **Hyeseong Kim** (50.09) for the final spots. While **Hyeseong Kim** projected respectably, the optimizer penalized him heavily for an assumed #9 spot in the order and the restrictive influence of an elite opposing catcher\u2019s pop time. Consequently, **Hyeseong Kim** falls to the bench, as his path to accumulating counting stats appears narrow compared to the heart-of-the-order opportunities for **Colt Keith** and **Kazuma Okamoto** (55.40). Meanwhile, **Trea Turner** (50.80) and **Yordan Alvarez** (54.86) remain in the lineup despite the tough assignment against Paul Skenes and Nathan Eovaldi, respectively; their raw talent ceiling outweighs the efficiency hit taken from facing two of the league\u2019s premier arms. Note that **Zac Veen** and **Caleb Durbin** remain unavailable for selection, as we continue to monitor the minors and active roster status for any movement that could bolster our category depth.",
